A definition with the ring-theoretic identifications recorded from the cited suppliers. $A_0=\mathbb Q[q]_{(q)}$ is the localisation of $\mathbb Q[q]$ away from the multiplicative set $\mathbb Q[q]\setminus(q)$, hence a local ring with maximal ideal $qA_0$; [[ex-the-local-ring-of-polynomials-at-x]] realises it as the ring of rational functions $f/g$ with $g(0)
eq0$ and identifies the residue field with $\mathbb Q$ by evaluation at $0$, because $\mathbb Q[q]/(q)\cong\mathbb Q$ and localising at a prime yields a local ring whose maximal ideal is the extended prime ([[def-localisation-at-a-prime-ideal]]). A localisation of the principal ideal domain $\mathbb Q[q]$ at a maximal ideal is a discrete valuation ring, hence a principal ideal domain ([[def-principal-ideal-domain]], [[def-discrete-valuation-ring]], [[cor-dvr-is-a-pid]]); localisation does not change the fraction field, so it is $\mathbb Q(q)$. Units: $f/g$ is invertible in the localisation exactly when $f(0)
eq0$ as well, since the non-units form the maximal ideal $qA_0$. For a free $A_0$-module $L= igoplus_{j\in J}A_0e_j$ the displayed map sends $1\otimes e_j$ to $e_j+qL$, and both sides are free over $\mathbb Q$ with these families as bases, using $A_0/qA_0\cong\mathbb Q$; scaling $L$ by $\lambda$ multiplies $qL$ by $\lambda$ as well. The comparisons with $A$ are the explicit computations $q^{-1}
otin A_0$ and $(1+q)^{-1}
otin A$ ([[def-the-laurent-polynomial-ring]]). No choice principle is used: all objects are localisations and residue projections of explicitly presented rings.

items.statement:
Work in the two-dimensional simple $U_q(\mathfrak{sl}_2)$-module $V(\omega)=L_1(1)$ with basis $v$ (weight $\omega$) and $w=F_1v$ (weight $\omega-\alpha_1$), so that $\tilde f_1v=w$, $\tilde e_1w=v$, $\tilde e_1v=0$, $\tilde f_1w=0$ and $\mathcal L(\omega)=A_0v\oplus A_0w$ is a crystal lattice with crystal basis $\{v+q\mathcal L(\omega),\,w+q\mathcal L(\omega)\}$ ([[thm-quantized-sl-two-string-formulas]](iii), [[def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity]], [[def-crystal-lattice-and-crystal-basis]]).

Let $$L:=A_0(v+w)\oplus A_0(qv)\subseteq V(\omega).$$ Then:
(a) $L$ is a free $A_0$-module of rank $2$ and generates $V(\omega)$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$;
(b) $L$ is not stable under $\tilde e_1$: $\tilde e_1(v+w)=v\notin L$;
(c) $L$ is not weight-decomposed: $L_\omega=L\cap\mathbb Q(q)v=A_0qv$ and $L_{\omega-\alpha_1}=L\cap\mathbb Q(q)w=qA_0w$, but $v+w\notin L_\omega\oplus L_{\omega-\alpha_1}$, so $L\ne L_\omega\oplus L_{\omega-\alpha_1}$.

Consequently there is no set $B$ for which $(L,B)$ is a crystal basis of $V(\omega)$: a crystal basis requires $L$ to be a crystal lattice, i.e. to satisfy [[def-crystal-lattice-and-crystal-basis]] (i)-(iii), and (b) violates (iii) while (c) violates (ii). In particular the axioms (ii) and (iii) are not automatic for a free lattice generating the module, and they are not implied by one another. No choice principle is used.
